   "id": "h2-14",
   "name": "Functions of fibre in the diet"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Although it is not digested, fibre is important:\n• It adds bulk and holds water, so faeces are soft and pass easily: it prevents constipation.\n• It helps to prevent piles (haemorrhoids), diverticular disease and cancer of the bowel.\n• It makes us feel full, which helps to control body weight.\n• It slows the absorption of sugar (helpful in diabetes) and helps lower blood cholesterol (protecting the heart).\n• It feeds useful bacteria in the large intestine.\nEat fibre with plenty of water. Too little fibre in a diet of refined foods leads to constipation.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "n": "Ayuk"
  },
  "problem": "{n} eats white bread, white rice and meat, and often has constipation. Explain and advise him.",
  "steps": [
   "His diet is low in fibre: white bread and white rice are refined, and meat has no fibre.",
   "Fibre adds bulk and holds water, keeping faeces soft.",
   "He should eat whole grains, beans, vegetables and fruit, and drink enough water."
  ],
  "answer": "Too little fibre; eat whole grains, beans, vegetables, fruit, and drink water.",
  "checks": []
 },
